03-10
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.705
The activities form this week's lesson.

04-01
00:02:44.235 --> 00:02:51.041
Pause here for about eight minutes and complete questions A one
through A three on worksheet page one.

04-02
00:02:51.221 --> 00:02:53.532
First, classify the examples.

04-03
00:02:53.712 --> 00:02:59.301
Ask whether material moves between bulk phases or accumulates at a
surface.

04-04
00:02:59.481 --> 00:03:08.527
Then identify the constraint that makes a rigid closed vessel
different from the fixed-temperature, fixed-pressure examples in Week
three.

04-05
00:03:08.707 --> 00:03:17.667
For the inert-gas question, write a sentence distinguishing the
pressure contributed by the subliming species from the total mixture
pressure.

04-06
00:03:17.847 --> 00:03:21.068
You do not need numerical values yet.

04-07
00:03:21.248 --> 00:03:26.944
The purpose is to establish which physical quantities the later
equations describe.

04-08
00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:30.111
Keep your answers even if you are unsure.

04-09
00:03:30.291 --> 00:03:36.477
Use the next slide as a check only after you have made an attempt, and
explain any correction.

05-01
00:03:39.007 --> 00:03:40.906
Check your classifications.

05-02
00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:47.763
Dissolution of a crystal and sublimation transfer the same substance
between bulk phases.

05-03
00:03:47.943 --> 00:03:51.997
Adsorption describes uptake associated with a surface.

05-04
00:03:52.177 --> 00:03:59.409
A chemical reaction would instead change species identities and
require additional reaction constraints.

05-05
00:03:59.589 --> 00:04:03.493
The relevant energy criterion depends on what is fixed.

05-06
00:04:03.673 --> 00:04:08.942
At fixed temperature and pressure, a closed system minimizes Gibbs
energy.

05-07
00:04:09.122 --> 00:04:14.903
At fixed temperature and volume, the corresponding potential is
Helmholtz energy.

05-08
00:04:15.083 --> 00:04:21.867
In both cases, conserved inventories and the allowed states remain
constraints on the minimization.

05-09
00:04:22.047 --> 00:04:27.125
For an ideal gas mixture, total pressure is the sum of partial
pressures.

05-10
00:04:27.305 --> 00:04:34.430
An inert gas can therefore raise total pressure without changing the
amount of the subliming species.

05-11
00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.346
Keep that distinction visible when we later interpret the Lab output.

06-01
00:04:41.876 --> 00:04:45.823
We begin with a solid and a liquid at equilibrium.

06-02
00:04:46.003 --> 00:04:51.400
The chemical potential of the component must be the same in the two
present phases.

06-03
00:04:51.580 --> 00:05:01.457
On the liquid side, we write its chemical potential as the pure-liquid
reference plus R T times the natural logarithm of x times gamma.

06-04
00:05:01.637 --> 00:05:06.608
The reference is the subcooled pure liquid at the same temperature and
pressure.

06-05
00:05:06.788 --> 00:05:15.872
That reference may be a hypothetical continuation below the melting
temperature, but it defines the activity convention consistently.

06-06
00:05:16.052 --> 00:05:20.404
The pure solid has unit activity on its own standard state.

06-07
00:05:20.584 --> 00:05:26.216
Unit activity does not mean that its chemical potential or amount is
zero.

06-08
00:05:26.396 --> 00:05:29.404
The equality determines a liquid activity.

06-09
00:05:29.584 --> 00:05:35.579
Determining how many moles occupy each phase still requires a material
balance.

07-01
00:05:38.109 --> 00:05:43.143
The solubility equation links liquid activity to fusion properties.

07-02
00:05:43.323 --> 00:05:54.395
Read the right-hand side as negative fusion enthalpy divided by R,
multiplied by the quantity one over T minus one over the melting
temperature.

07-03
00:05:54.575 --> 00:05:57.455
That entire expression is dimensionless.

07-04
00:05:57.635 --> 00:06:02.627
Use temperature in kelvin and fusion enthalpy in joules per mole.

07-05
00:06:02.807 --> 00:06:07.671
With R in joules per mole per kelvin, the units cancel correctly.

07-06
00:06:07.851 --> 00:06:15.745
The expression assumes constant fusion enthalpy and neglects the heat-
capacity correction between solid and liquid.

07-07
00:06:15.925 --> 00:06:22.645
It also assumes the specified pure solid, without solid solutions or
chemical reactions.

07-08
00:06:22.825 --> 00:06:27.795
Below the melting temperature, the reciprocal-temperature difference
is positive.

07-09
00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:32.242
The negative sign therefore gives a negative logarithm.

07-10
00:06:32.422 --> 00:06:37.777
For an ideal liquid, that predicts a saturated mole fraction below
one.

08-01
00:06:40.307 --> 00:06:46.088
Pause for about six minutes and complete B one and B two on worksheet
page one.

08-02
00:06:46.268 --> 00:06:50.791
The synthetic melting temperature is three hundred and fifty kelvin.

08-03
00:06:50.971 --> 00:06:58.143
The fusion enthalpy is ten thousand joules per mole, and the system
temperature is three hundred kelvin.

08-04
00:06:58.323 --> 00:07:01.651
Assume an ideal liquid, so gamma is one.

08-05
00:07:01.831 --> 00:07:10.322
First calculate the exponent in the solubility equation, then take the
exponential to obtain the saturated mole fraction.

08-06
00:07:10.502 --> 00:07:15.430
Write the intermediate value so you can check the sign and units
later.

08-07
00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:21.327
For B two, decide whether the resulting number gives moles of
remaining solid.

08-08
00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:27.139
Explain what additional inventory or composition information you would
need.

08-09
00:07:27.319 --> 00:07:32.823
Make that distinction before continuing to the numerical check on the
next slide.

09-01
00:07:35.353 --> 00:07:41.135
The exponent is approximately negative zero point five seven two seven
three.

09-02
00:07:41.315 --> 00:07:48.803
Its exponential gives a saturated liquid mole fraction of about zero
point five six four.

09-03
00:07:48.983 --> 00:07:52.289
This is a composition within the liquid phase.

09-04
00:07:52.469 --> 00:08:00.789
It does not tell us the number of moles dissolved, the total amount of
liquid, or the remaining solid inventory.

09-05
00:08:00.969 --> 00:08:07.348
For a nonideal liquid, the same thermodynamic relation constrains x
times gamma.

09-06
00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:16.872
Since gamma can depend on composition, finding the composition may
require solving an implicit equation within the admitted phase model.

09-07
00:08:17.052 --> 00:08:21.233
We still need material balances to determine phase amounts.

09-08
00:08:21.413 --> 00:08:27.984
A eutectic introduces additional solid-phase conditions and a second
solubility branch.

09-09
00:08:28.164 --> 00:08:35.595
That extension is available in the optional appendix, but it is not
required for today's investigation.

10-01
00:08:38.125 --> 00:08:42.499
Now consider a pure bulk solid in equilibrium with its vapor.

10-02
00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:47.607
Equality of chemical potential applies while both phases are present.

10-03
00:08:47.787 --> 00:08:57.984
With the ideal-gas and solid-pressure approximations stated here, this
gives a vapor partial pressure equal to the sublimation pressure at
that temperature.

10-04
00:08:58.164 --> 00:09:00.980
The phrase while solid remains matters.

10-05
00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:08.776
A small initial solid inventory may completely enter the gas phase
before the vapor reaches that pressure.

10-06
00:09:08.956 --> 00:09:17.468
The final state can then have a lower partial pressure because there
is no bulk solid left to enforce the two-phase equality.

10-07
00:09:17.648 --> 00:09:20.784
Also note which pressure appears in the condition.

10-08
00:09:20.964 --> 00:09:24.761
It is the partial pressure of the subliming species.

10-09
00:09:24.941 --> 00:09:32.233
An inert gas contributes to total pressure, but it is not part of that
species' sublimation inventory.

11-01
00:09:34.763 --> 00:09:40.950
Start the vessel calculation by finding the gas capacity at
sublimation equilibrium.

11-02
00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:47.423
Multiply sublimation pressure by accessible gas volume, then divide by
R T.

11-03
00:09:47.603 --> 00:09:52.617
Compare that capacity with the total amount of the subliming species.

11-04
00:09:52.797 --> 00:09:58.301
Gas moles equal the smaller of the available inventory and that
capacity.

11-05
00:09:58.481 --> 00:10:01.766
Solid moles are the remaining inventory.

11-06
00:10:01.946 --> 00:10:13.039
Use gas moles in the ideal-gas equation to obtain the species partial
pressure, and add the inert contribution only when calculating total
pressure.

11-07
00:10:13.219 --> 00:10:21.945
With kilopascals and liters, R has the numerical value eight point
three one four four six in compatible units.

11-08
00:10:22.125 --> 00:10:34.007
The gas chemical potential minus the pure-solid reference, divided by
R T, is the natural logarithm of partial pressure divided by
sublimation pressure.

11-09
00:10:34.187 --> 00:10:36.769
Zero applies with present solid.

11-10
00:10:36.949 --> 00:10:41.002
A negative value is allowed after solid exhaustion.

12-01
00:10:43.532 --> 00:10:50.913
Follow the worked case at three hundred kelvin, one liter and a
sublimation pressure of ten kilopascals.

12-02
00:10:51.093 --> 00:10:56.256
The capacity is ten times one, divided by R times three hundred.

12-03
00:10:56.436 --> 00:11:02.175
This gives approximately zero point zero zero four zero zero nine
moles.

12-04
00:11:02.355 --> 00:11:08.200
An inventory of zero point zero one zero moles exceeds the capacity.

12-05
00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:18.343
About zero point zero zero five nine nine one moles therefore remain
solid, and the vapor partial pressure is ten kilopascals.

12-06
00:11:18.523 --> 00:11:24.112
An inventory of zero point zero zero two moles is below capacity.

12-07
00:11:24.292 --> 00:11:30.308
All of it enters the gas phase, giving about four point nine eight
nine kilopascals.

12-08
00:11:30.488 --> 00:11:40.003
Adding zero point zero zero two moles of inert gas adds the same
pressure contribution at this fixed temperature and volume.

13-01
00:11:42.533 --> 00:11:48.997
Now pause for about ten minutes and complete C one through C three on
worksheet page two.

13-02
00:11:49.177 --> 00:11:56.835
This is a different vessel: two liters at three hundred kelvin, with a
sublimation pressure of eight kilopascals.

13-03
00:11:57.015 --> 00:12:04.781
Compare total inventories of zero point zero one zero and zero point
zero zero four moles.

13-04
00:12:04.961 --> 00:12:11.809
Calculate the capacity first, then gas amount, remaining solid and
species partial pressure.

13-05
00:12:11.989 --> 00:12:19.327
Add zero point zero zero one mole of inert gas to each case and
calculate total pressure.

13-06
00:12:19.507 --> 00:12:26.526
Finally, calculate the natural logarithm of partial pressure divided
by sublimation pressure.

13-07
00:12:26.706 --> 00:12:35.410
Explain the difference between the equality condition for a present
solid and a valid state at the exhausted-solid boundary.

13-08
00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:39.707
Show your substitutions and units before opening the check.

14-01
00:12:42.237 --> 00:12:48.659
The gas capacity is approximately zero point zero zero six four one
five moles.

14-02
00:12:48.839 --> 00:13:00.871
Therefore, the larger inventory retains about zero point zero zero
three five eight five moles of solid and has a species partial
pressure of eight kilopascals.

14-03
00:13:01.051 --> 00:13:08.155
The smaller inventory completely vaporizes and gives about four point
nine eight nine kilopascals.

14-04
00:13:08.335 --> 00:13:13.647
The inert contributes about one point two four seven kilopascals in
each case.

14-05
00:13:13.827 --> 00:13:18.819
Add that contribution to obtain the total pressures shown in the
table.

14-06
00:13:18.999 --> 00:13:30.579
The logarithmic chemical-potential difference is zero with solid
present, and about negative zero point four seven two three for the
exhausted-solid case.

14-07
00:13:30.759 --> 00:13:33.575
A negative value is appropriate there.

14-08
00:13:33.755 --> 00:13:39.301
Check that gas plus solid equals the volatile-species inventory in
each row.

15-01
00:13:41.831 --> 00:13:46.610
Pause for about five minutes and complete D one on worksheet page
three.

15-02
00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:53.250
Read the first two pages of the Lab guide, but make your predictions
before running the simulation.

15-03
00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:58.017
We will use three cases at three hundred kelvin and one liter.

15-04
00:13:58.197 --> 00:14:03.893
Set the sublimation reference pressure to ten kilopascals at three
hundred kelvin.

15-05
00:14:04.073 --> 00:14:11.091
Case ten A has zero point zero one zero moles of volatile species and
no inert.

15-06
00:14:11.271 --> 00:14:17.138
Case ten B reduces that inventory to zero point zero zero two moles.

15-07
00:14:17.318 --> 00:14:25.915
Case ten C keeps the smaller volatile inventory and adds zero point
zero zero two moles of inert gas.

15-08
00:14:26.095 --> 00:14:30.938
Predict phase presence, partial pressure and total pressure for each
case.

15-09
00:14:31.118 --> 00:14:35.683
Identify the one input changed between successive cases.

16-01
00:14:38.213 --> 00:14:41.307
We are now in the real Lab ten interface.

16-02
00:14:41.487 --> 00:14:46.713
Keep worksheet page three and Lab guide pages one and two beside the
browser.

16-03
00:14:46.893 --> 00:14:53.315
In the Experiment menu, choose gas solid equilibrium, sublimation in a
rigid vessel.

16-04
00:14:53.495 --> 00:14:57.505
The lab initially supplies its own synthetic defaults.

16-05
00:14:57.685 --> 00:15:02.699
Those initial results are not our worksheet case, so do not copy them.

16-06
00:15:02.879 --> 00:15:06.825
Selecting an experiment may also reset the controls.

16-07
00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:11.677
Choose the experiment first, then enter every value in the guide.

16-08
00:15:11.857 --> 00:15:18.535
Our first record will be case ten A, with enough volatile material for
some solid to remain.

16-09
00:15:18.715 --> 00:15:24.496
Enter a temperature of three hundred kelvin and a free gas volume of
one liter.

16-10
00:15:24.676 --> 00:15:32.740
Set the total volatile inventory to zero point zero one zero mole and
the inert gas inventory to zero.

16-11
00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.141
These are total amounts, not mole fractions.

16-12
00:15:36.321 --> 00:15:40.652
The free gas volume is the space available to the gas.

16-13
00:15:40.832 --> 00:15:44.715
In this teaching model we neglect the solid volume.

16-14
00:15:44.895 --> 00:15:50.953
Keep the declared temperature interval from two hundred fifty to three
hundred fifty kelvin.

16-15
00:15:51.133 --> 00:16:00.328
The source field identifies these numbers as synthetic teaching
definitions rather than measured properties of a real chemical.

16-16
00:16:00.508 --> 00:16:04.305
The reference temperature is also three hundred kelvin.

16-17
00:16:04.485 --> 00:16:13.168
Enter a reference sublimation pressure of ten kilopascals and a
sublimation enthalpy of fifty thousand joules per mole.

16-18
00:16:13.348 --> 00:16:19.321
At the reference temperature the correlation therefore returns ten
kilopascals directly.

16-19
00:16:19.501 --> 00:16:24.088
The enthalpy controls the temperature dependence away from that point.

16-20
00:16:24.268 --> 00:16:32.417
For real properties, the declared interval must exclude melting and
solid transitions and match the source correlation.

16-21
00:16:32.597 --> 00:16:38.101
Notice that changing inputs clears the previous results and disables
export.

16-22
00:16:38.281 --> 00:16:43.231
Press Calculate equilibrium only after checking the values and units.

16-23
00:16:43.411 --> 00:16:46.867
The result for case ten A is solid plus gas.

16-24
00:16:47.047 --> 00:16:56.028
The volatile partial pressure and the total pressure both equal ten
kilopascals because this case contains no inert gas.

16-25
00:16:56.208 --> 00:16:58.384
Read the two amounts separately.

16-26
00:16:58.564 --> 00:17:09.935
There are about zero point zero zero four zero zero nine mole of
volatile gas and zero point zero zero five nine nine one mole of
solid.

16-27
00:17:10.115 --> 00:17:14.253
Their sum is the entered zero point zero one zero mole.

16-28
00:17:14.433 --> 00:17:19.532
The chemical potential difference is zero within numerical precision.

16-29
00:17:19.712 --> 00:17:23.893
That equality is appropriate because both phases are present.

16-30
00:17:24.073 --> 00:17:34.036
A small balance residual is a useful calculation check, but it does
not validate the physical assumptions for an unknown real material.

16-31
00:17:34.216 --> 00:17:37.715
Change the View menu to Gas and solid amounts.

16-32
00:17:37.895 --> 00:17:42.055
Read the horizontal axis before interpreting the curves.

16-33
00:17:42.235 --> 00:17:46.459
It is temperature, not time and not added inventory.

16-34
00:17:46.639 --> 00:17:54.511
Each point is a separate equilibrium calculation at that temperature
with the same total volatile inventory.

16-35
00:17:54.691 --> 00:17:59.704
As temperature rises, more of the volatile material can enter the gas.

16-36
00:17:59.884 --> 00:18:05.964
Eventually the solid amount reaches zero and all the volatile material
is gaseous.

16-37
00:18:06.144 --> 00:18:11.797
The gas and solid amounts sum to the same inventory throughout the
admitted range.

16-38
00:18:11.977 --> 00:18:16.095
This plot does not tell us how quickly sublimation occurs.

16-39
00:18:16.275 --> 00:18:20.755
Our selected operating point remains three hundred kelvin.

16-40
00:18:20.935 --> 00:18:29.148
Now change only the total volatile inventory to zero point zero zero
two mole and calculate again.

16-41
00:18:29.328 --> 00:18:31.248
This is case ten B.

16-42
00:18:31.428 --> 00:18:35.503
The heading changes to unsaturated gas, no solid.

16-43
00:18:35.683 --> 00:18:44.387
All the volatile material is now gas, and the volatile partial
pressure is about four point nine eight nine kilopascals.

16-44
00:18:44.567 --> 00:18:51.863
The sublimation pressure remains ten kilopascals because the
temperature and correlation have not changed.

16-45
00:18:52.043 --> 00:18:56.459
There simply is not enough material to saturate this vessel.

16-46
00:18:56.639 --> 00:19:02.783
Do not force the gas pressure to ten kilopascals or report a negative
solid amount.

16-47
00:19:02.963 --> 00:19:10.408
The negative gas minus solid chemical potential difference is
compatible with the solid being absent.

16-48
00:19:10.588 --> 00:19:21.468
For case ten C, keep the volatile inventory at zero point zero zero
two mole and add zero point zero zero two mole of inert gas.

16-49
00:19:21.648 --> 00:19:26.149
Calculate again without changing temperature or free volume.

16-50
00:19:26.329 --> 00:19:38.357
The volatile partial pressure remains about four point nine eight nine
kilopascals, but the total gas pressure becomes about nine point nine
seven seven kilopascals.

16-51
00:19:38.537 --> 00:19:41.716
The volatile gas mole fraction is one half.

16-52
00:19:41.896 --> 00:19:43.923
No solid has reappeared.

16-53
00:19:44.103 --> 00:19:55.260
The inert gas contributes its own ideal partial pressure without
changing the volatile inventory or its saturation capacity under these
assumptions.

16-54
00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:00.197
Always identify whether a reported pressure is partial or total.

16-55
00:20:00.377 --> 00:20:05.455
In the pressure view, distinguish the three curves using their legend
labels.

16-56
00:20:05.635 --> 00:20:18.712
The sublimation pressure is a reference property at each temperature,
the actual volatile partial pressure obeys the finite inventory
constraint, and the total pressure includes the inert gas.

16-57
00:20:18.892 --> 00:20:27.255
At low temperatures, enough volatile material can remain solid for the
partial pressure to follow the sublimation curve.

16-58
00:20:27.435 --> 00:20:33.259
After the solid is exhausted, the partial pressure falls below that
reference curve.

16-59
00:20:33.439 --> 00:20:38.281
These are equilibrium states across temperature, not a time record.

16-60
00:20:38.461 --> 00:20:43.731
Use the numerical table for precise values at three hundred kelvin.

16-61
00:20:43.911 --> 00:20:47.196
Keep a separate record for each calculated case.

16-62
00:20:47.376 --> 00:20:54.480
In Your interpretation, write the phase label, the volatile balance,
and the reason for any pressure change.

16-63
00:20:54.660 --> 00:21:02.111
Expand Study workspace, reopen and compare cases, and enter a clear
label for the next captured case.

16-64
00:21:02.291 --> 00:21:07.219
Use Export study JSON after calculating that case.

16-65
00:21:07.399 --> 00:21:14.333
The export downloads the inputs and results and also retains the case
in this temporary comparison.

16-66
00:21:14.513 --> 00:21:21.126
Here the three labels distinguish solid remaining, solid exhausted,
and inert gas added.

16-67
00:21:21.306 --> 00:21:28.026
Saving current inputs only is a different action and does not contain
a calculated result.

16-68
00:21:28.206 --> 00:21:33.390
Select Download comparison to preserve all three cases in one file.

16-69
00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:39.394
The comparison uses recorded field names, and some values use internal
units.

16-70
00:21:39.574 --> 00:21:50.753
For example, the gas solid calculation stores pressure in pascals and
volume in cubic meters, whereas the main controls display kilopascals
and liters.

16-71
00:21:50.933 --> 00:21:54.410
Ten thousand pascals is ten kilopascals.

16-72
00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:59.070
Compare quantities only after checking their units and physical
meaning.

16-73
00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:04.002
The comparison is recorded evidence, not a fresh calculation.

16-74
00:22:04.182 --> 00:22:10.091
Keep the downloaded file with your worksheet so that you can reproduce
the result later.

16-75
00:22:10.271 --> 00:22:15.626
We will now reopen the saved file from the beginning of the restore
workflow.

16-76
00:22:15.806 --> 00:22:23.635
Because our three synthetic cases have already been downloaded, I
clear only this temporary comparison from memory.

16-77
00:22:23.815 --> 00:22:26.354
The downloaded file is unchanged.

16-78
00:22:26.534 --> 00:22:34.576
Under Load studies or a comparison JSON, choose the solids comparison
file that was just saved.

16-79
00:22:34.756 --> 00:22:41.284
You can instead select an individual study JSON if you only need one
case.

16-80
00:22:41.464 --> 00:22:44.878
Loading a file should produce case labels to restore.

16-81
00:22:45.058 --> 00:22:50.882
It does not mean the main calculator has already recalculated the
imported inputs.

16-82
00:22:51.062 --> 00:22:55.520
The three case labels have returned after loading the comparison.

16-83
00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:59.711
Select Restore beside case ten A, solid remains.

16-84
00:22:59.891 --> 00:23:11.240
Check that the controls return to one liter, zero point zero one zero
mole of volatile material, zero inert gas, and the correct reference
pressure.

16-85
00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:14.983
The stored interpretation should return with the inputs.

16-86
00:23:15.163 --> 00:23:20.880
Keep the distinction between opening a recorded result and solving the
current state.

16-87
00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:27.887
In particular, switching the experiment during restoration can briefly
display default results.

16-88
00:23:28.067 --> 00:23:33.315
Always finish restoration by pressing Calculate equilibrium again.

16-89
00:23:33.495 --> 00:23:46.120
The recalculated case again reports solid plus gas, a volatile partial
pressure of ten kilopascals, and the same gas and solid amounts as our
original record.

16-90
00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:49.543
That is the reproducibility check we wanted.

16-91
00:23:49.723 --> 00:23:59.764
In worksheet section D, retain the three rows, your independent
material balance, and one explanation of partial versus total
pressure.

16-92
00:23:59.944 --> 00:24:04.296
Use the next activity stop to repeat this workflow yourself.

16-93
00:24:04.476 --> 00:24:16.146
Your evidence should show that you can identify which phase is
present, apply the finite inventory constraint, and reopen the inputs
without relying on a screenshot alone.

17-01
00:24:18.676 --> 00:24:22.942
Use this list to decide what evidence belongs in your record.

17-02
00:24:23.122 --> 00:24:28.772
Start with the active mode and inputs, because they define the problem
being solved.

17-03
00:24:28.952 --> 00:24:36.248
Next, record the gas amount and remaining solid, which establish
whether both bulk phases are present.

17-04
00:24:36.428 --> 00:24:40.204
Keep species partial pressure separate from total pressure.

17-05
00:24:40.384 --> 00:24:48.490
Then check the volatile inventory independently: gas moles plus solid
moles must equal the volatile total.

17-06
00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:51.358
The inert has its own conserved amount.

17-07
00:24:51.538 --> 00:24:54.717
The Lab plots sweep temperature, not time.

17-08
00:24:54.897 --> 00:25:01.340
The volatile partial pressure follows the sublimation-pressure curve
wherever solid remains.

17-09
00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:09.882
A crossing or boundary on that plot describes a change across the
stated conditions, rather than a measured time history.

17-10
00:25:10.062 --> 00:25:14.862
Save the study file so that the chosen conditions can be reproduced.

18-01
00:25:17.392 --> 00:25:23.494
Pause for about twenty-two minutes and complete D two through D four
on worksheet page three.

18-02
00:25:23.674 --> 00:25:28.644
Open Lab ten using the link, and follow Lab guide pages one and two.

18-03
00:25:28.824 --> 00:25:31.960
Run all three specified cases yourself.

18-04
00:25:32.140 --> 00:25:38.028
Record phase presence, gas and solid amounts, partial pressure and
total pressure.

18-05
00:25:38.208 --> 00:25:45.803
Use your calculator to check the capacity and the inert contribution,
rather than copying the output alone.

18-06
00:25:45.983 --> 00:25:52.106
Inspect the temperature plots and explain where the finite inventory
becomes important.

18-07
00:25:52.286 --> 00:25:55.848
Export your studies and reopen one saved file.

18-08
00:25:56.028 --> 00:26:00.995
Restore its controls, verify their values, and calculate again.

18-09
00:26:01.175 --> 00:26:04.418
Confirm that the result agrees with the saved case.

18-10
00:26:04.598 --> 00:26:08.523
Keep the comparison table and the calculation evidence.

18-11
00:26:08.703 --> 00:26:13.994
If a result differs from your prediction, explain the difference
before moving on.

19-01
00:26:16.524 --> 00:26:19.020
Check the three cases against the table.

19-02
00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:30.186
Case ten A retains about zero point zero zero five nine nine one moles
of solid, so its volatile partial pressure is ten kilopascals.

19-03
00:26:30.366 --> 00:26:37.193
Case ten B has no solid left and gives about four point nine eight
nine kilopascals.

19-04
00:26:37.373 --> 00:26:45.160
Case ten C has the same volatile inventory and the same volatile
partial pressure as case ten B.

19-05
00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:52.529
The added inert gas increases total pressure to about nine point nine
seven seven kilopascals.

19-06
00:26:52.709 --> 00:26:59.813
This is a direct example of why total pressure cannot replace partial
pressure in the sublimation condition.

19-07
00:26:59.993 --> 00:27:04.793
The values describe equilibrium endpoints under the stated model.

19-08
00:27:04.973 --> 00:27:13.250
They do not predict a sublimation rate, a transport resistance or the
time needed for the vessel to reach equilibrium.

20-01
00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.850
Take a ten-minute break here.

20-02
00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:22.531
Save your Lab ten files and your worksheet before leaving the screen.

20-03
00:27:22.711 --> 00:27:26.359
There is no calculation to complete during this break.

20-04
00:27:26.539 --> 00:27:31.936
When you return, open worksheet page four and Lab guide pages three
and four.

20-05
00:27:32.116 --> 00:27:35.956
We will move from bulk sublimation to surface adsorption.

20-06
00:27:36.136 --> 00:27:41.427
That change requires a new variable: uptake per unit mass of
adsorbent.

20-07
00:27:41.607 --> 00:27:45.319
The material-balance question will remain familiar.

20-08
00:27:45.499 --> 00:27:56.464
We will first evaluate an equilibrium relation at a specified
pressure, then determine the pressure that can exist in a closed
vessel with a fixed inventory.

20-09
00:27:56.644 --> 00:28:01.380
Keep the distinction between a state quantity and an amount in mind.

20-10
00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:05.422
Resume when you are ready to begin the adsorption section.

21-01
00:28:07.952 --> 00:28:12.112
Adsorption describes material associated with a surface.

21-02
00:28:12.292 --> 00:28:19.289
An adsorption isotherm gives uptake as a function of gas pressure at a
specified temperature.

21-03
00:28:19.469 --> 00:28:25.592
In this lesson, q is absolute uptake measured in moles per kilogram of
adsorbent.

21-04
00:28:25.772 --> 00:28:29.185
The pressure is the partial pressure of the adsorbate.

21-05
00:28:29.365 --> 00:28:36.746
To turn uptake into an amount in moles, multiply q by the adsorbent
mass expressed in kilograms.

21-06
00:28:36.926 --> 00:28:46.630
For example, a value of one mole per kilogram on ten grams of
adsorbent corresponds to zero point zero one mole adsorbed.

21-07
00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:50.650
That distinction will matter when we change adsorbent mass.

21-08
00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:57.294
A lower uptake per kilogram does not automatically mean fewer total
adsorbed moles.

21-09
00:28:57.474 --> 00:29:02.808
The total amount depends on both uptake and the amount of adsorbent
present.

22-01
00:29:05.338 --> 00:29:14.383
The Langmuir equation writes uptake as the saturation uptake
multiplied by b P, divided by one plus b P.

22-02
00:29:14.563 --> 00:29:18.616
Saturation uptake has units of moles per kilogram.

22-03
00:29:18.796 --> 00:29:24.940
The parameter b has reciprocal-pressure units, so the product b P is
dimensionless.

22-04
00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:31.158
The ratio of uptake to saturation uptake is the fractional coverage in
this model.

22-05
00:29:31.338 --> 00:29:35.476
It rises from zero toward one as pressure increases.

22-06
00:29:35.656 --> 00:29:39.411
At b P equal to one, the coverage is one half.

22-07
00:29:39.591 --> 00:29:47.378
The idealized interpretation involves a finite set of equivalent sites
without lateral interactions.

22-08
00:29:47.558 --> 00:29:56.855
These assumptions help us understand the equation, but fitting the
equation successfully does not establish a microscopic mechanism.

22-09
00:29:57.035 --> 00:30:04.224
Later, we will use it as a specified synthetic model and check its
consequences through a vessel balance.

23-01
00:30:06.754 --> 00:30:11.064
At sufficiently low pressure, b P is much smaller than one.

23-02
00:30:11.244 --> 00:30:20.417
The denominator of the Langmuir equation is then approximately one, so
uptake becomes saturation uptake times b times pressure.

23-03
00:30:20.597 --> 00:30:27.189
This gives the Henry adsorption coefficient as saturation uptake
multiplied by b.

23-04
00:30:27.369 --> 00:30:33.854
With our chosen units, that coefficient is measured in moles per
kilogram per kilopascal.

23-05
00:30:34.034 --> 00:30:40.648
A matched Henry line and Langmuir curve therefore share the same slope
as pressure approaches zero.

23-06
00:30:40.828 --> 00:30:44.284
They do not necessarily agree at finite pressure.

23-07
00:30:44.464 --> 00:30:55.066
The word Henry also appears in gas-solubility equations, sometimes
written as partial pressure equals a constant times liquid mole
fraction.

23-08
00:30:55.246 --> 00:30:58.489
That convention defines a different constant.

23-09
00:30:58.669 --> 00:31:05.816
Always state the equation, phases and concentration basis rather than
relying on the name alone.

24-01
00:31:08.346 --> 00:31:16.367
Use the synthetic saturation uptake of two moles per kilogram and b
equal to zero point one per kilopascal.

24-02
00:31:16.547 --> 00:31:23.822
Their product gives a Henry adsorption coefficient of zero point two
moles per kilogram per kilopascal.

24-03
00:31:24.002 --> 00:31:27.884
At one kilopascal, b P is zero point one.

24-04
00:31:28.064 --> 00:31:36.658
The Langmuir uptake is about zero point one eight one eight moles per
kilogram, while the Henry value is zero point two.

24-05
00:31:36.838 --> 00:31:40.230
At ten kilopascals, b P equals one.

24-06
00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:47.087
Langmuir uptake is then one mole per kilogram, exactly half of its
saturation uptake.

24-07
00:31:47.267 --> 00:31:50.616
The Henry line predicts two moles per kilogram.

24-08
00:31:50.796 --> 00:31:55.682
This growing difference follows from the denominator in the Langmuir
equation.

24-09
00:31:55.862 --> 00:32:03.136
Ten kilopascals is already well beyond a convincing small-b-P
approximation for these parameters.

25-01
00:32:05.666 --> 00:32:11.469
Pause for about ten minutes and complete E one through E three on
worksheet page four.

25-02
00:32:11.649 --> 00:32:17.857
Keep the same synthetic parameters and compare pressures of five and
forty kilopascals.

25-03
00:32:18.037 --> 00:32:24.778
Calculate both Langmuir and matched Henry uptake, then calculate
Langmuir fractional coverage.

25-04
00:32:24.958 --> 00:32:28.670
Write the dimensionless product b P at each pressure.

25-05
00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:35.634
Use that product to judge the low-pressure assumption rather than
deciding from the pressure number alone.

25-06
00:32:35.814 --> 00:32:40.578
Finally, describe what each model predicts as pressure becomes large.

25-07
00:32:40.758 --> 00:32:51.680
Ask whether the equation contains a saturation limit and whether a
mathematical value outside that limit is physically credible for the
model's intended domain.

25-08
00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:54.228
Show the substitutions and units.

25-09
00:32:54.408 --> 00:33:00.318
Attempt the calculation before continuing to the comparison table on
the next slide.

26-01
00:33:02.848 --> 00:33:07.093
At five kilopascals, b P is zero point five.

26-02
00:33:07.273 --> 00:33:12.884
Langmuir uptake is two thirds of a mole per kilogram, with coverage
one third.

26-03
00:33:13.064 --> 00:33:16.818
The matched Henry line predicts one mole per kilogram.

26-04
00:33:16.998 --> 00:33:20.100
At forty kilopascals, b P is four.

26-05
00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:26.638
Langmuir uptake is one point six moles per kilogram and coverage is
zero point eight.

26-06
00:33:26.818 --> 00:33:30.252
The Henry value is eight moles per kilogram.

26-07
00:33:30.432 --> 00:33:39.307
That large value comes from extending a linear low-pressure expression
into a range where its assumption is not satisfied.

26-08
00:33:39.487 --> 00:33:47.679
Langmuir uptake approaches the specified saturation value of two moles
per kilogram as pressure increases.

26-09
00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:50.568
The Henry equation has no plateau.

26-10
00:33:50.748 --> 00:34:00.604
The comparison illustrates model domains, rather than evidence that a
real material actually follows either curve over this entire range.

27-01
00:34:03.134 --> 00:34:10.580
For a closed adsorption vessel, total adsorbate inventory equals gas
moles plus adsorbed moles.

27-02
00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:16.434
The gas term is pressure times accessible gas volume divided by R T.

27-03
00:34:16.614 --> 00:34:22.033
The adsorbed term is adsorbent mass multiplied by absolute uptake.

27-04
00:34:22.213 --> 00:34:31.578
Use the accessible gas volume in the ideal-gas term and convert
adsorbent mass to kilograms before multiplying by uptake.

27-05
00:34:31.758 --> 00:34:35.492
The unknown pressure is the adsorbate partial pressure.

27-06
00:34:35.672 --> 00:34:43.565
At fixed temperature, volume, mass and inventory, the isotherm and
this balance determine pressure together.

27-07
00:34:43.745 --> 00:34:52.705
For positive Langmuir parameters, both amount terms increase with
nonnegative pressure, giving a single nonnegative solution.

27-08
00:34:52.885 --> 00:34:55.466
The balance uses absolute uptake.

27-09
00:34:55.646 --> 00:35:03.113
An excess-adsorption measurement needs a density and volume convention
before it can replace that quantity.

28-01
00:35:05.643 --> 00:35:11.254
This worked example starts with a known pressure, rather than a known
total inventory.

28-02
00:35:11.434 --> 00:35:22.783
At ten kilopascals, with saturation uptake two and b equal to zero
point one per kilopascal, the Langmuir uptake is one mole per
kilogram.

28-03
00:35:22.963 --> 00:35:30.238
Ten grams is zero point zero one kilograms, so adsorbed moles equal
zero point zero one.

28-04
00:35:30.418 --> 00:35:37.607
The gas amount in one liter at three hundred kelvin is about four
point zero zero nine millimoles.

28-05
00:35:37.787 --> 00:35:46.064
Adding them gives a required total inventory of about zero point zero
one four zero zero nine moles.

28-06
00:35:46.244 --> 00:35:54.116
The next task reduces total inventory to zero point zero one mole
while keeping the other parameters fixed.

28-07
00:35:54.296 --> 00:35:58.456
Do not carry the ten-kilopascal pressure across unchanged.

28-08
00:35:58.636 --> 00:36:01.047
It must satisfy the new balance.

29-01
00:36:03.577 --> 00:36:09.593
Pause for about five minutes and complete F one through F three on
worksheet page four.

29-02
00:36:09.773 --> 00:36:14.701
Read Lab guide pages three and four before watching the demonstration.

29-03
00:36:14.881 --> 00:36:25.270
First predict the Langmuir pressure when the inventory is zero point
zero one mole, less than the amount needed for ten kilopascals in the
worked example.

29-04
00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:28.885
Next compare Langmuir with the matched Henry model.

29-05
00:36:29.065 --> 00:36:34.334
At the same positive pressure, the Henry expression predicts greater
uptake.

29-06
00:36:34.514 --> 00:36:42.130
Use the closed balance to decide which model should therefore require
a lower pressure for the same inventory.

29-07
00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:48.006
Finally, return to Langmuir and double adsorbent mass from ten to
twenty grams.

29-08
00:36:48.186 --> 00:36:52.880
Keep temperature, accessible volume and total inventory fixed.

29-09
00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:56.238
Predict the pressure direction and explain why.

30-01
00:36:58.768 --> 00:37:02.288
We are now in the real Lab eleven interface.

30-02
00:37:02.468 --> 00:37:07.268
Keep worksheet page five and Lab guide pages three and four open.

30-03
00:37:07.448 --> 00:37:11.182
Begin in Explore isotherms, the upper calculator.

30-04
00:37:11.362 --> 00:37:16.268
The data fitting section lower on the page is optional for this
lesson.

30-05
00:37:16.448 --> 00:37:22.123
Our main task is to compare two adsorption models and close a vessel
balance.

30-06
00:37:22.303 --> 00:37:30.576
The chart displays four model curves, but the model selected in the
menu is the one used to solve the closed vessel.

30-07
00:37:30.756 --> 00:37:36.303
The other curves are not additional phases and are not combined into
that balance.

30-08
00:37:36.483 --> 00:37:44.654
Select Langmuir and check a capacity of two moles per kilogram and an
affinity of zero point one per kilopascal.

30-09
00:37:44.834 --> 00:37:46.924
Use three hundred kelvin.

30-10
00:37:47.104 --> 00:37:53.184
The pressure units matter because the product of affinity and pressure
must be dimensionless.

30-11
00:37:53.364 --> 00:38:02.452
Entering a pressure in pascals while retaining an affinity per
kilopascal would change that product by a factor of one thousand.

30-12
00:38:02.632 --> 00:38:07.710
Identify the parameters as synthetic definitions at this temperature.

30-13
00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:14.524
Changing the temperature field does not generate a new temperature
dependent parameter set.

30-14
00:38:14.704 --> 00:38:24.731
For our first comparison, set the minimum pressure to zero and the
maximum to ten kilopascals so that the lower pressure curves are easy
to see.

30-15
00:38:24.911 --> 00:38:31.119
The worksheet uses zero to one hundred kilopascals for your
independent investigation.

30-16
00:38:31.299 --> 00:38:38.168
All three vessel roots are below ten kilopascals, so either admitted
window gives the same roots.

30-17
00:38:38.348 --> 00:38:40.652
We will widen the range later.

30-18
00:38:40.832 --> 00:38:45.440
Expand Closed vessel, fixed temperature and free gas volume.

30-19
00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:57.468
Enter one liter of free gas volume, ten grams of dry adsorbent, zero
point zero one zero mole of total adsorbate, and zero inert gas.

30-20
00:38:57.648 --> 00:39:02.405
This total inventory is the one used in the Lab investigation.

30-21
00:39:02.585 --> 00:39:12.740
It is different from the earlier worked example in which a pressure of
ten kilopascals was given and the required total inventory was
calculated.

30-22
00:39:12.920 --> 00:39:15.010
Here the pressure is unknown.

30-23
00:39:15.190 --> 00:39:21.313
The solver must divide the specified total amount between gas and
adsorbed material.

30-24
00:39:21.493 --> 00:39:25.440
Press Compare and solve vessel after checking these inputs.

30-25
00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:34.281
For case eleven A, the equilibrium adsorbate partial pressure is about
six point zero eight zero kilopascals.

30-26
00:39:34.461 --> 00:39:39.453
The uptake is about zero point seven five six two moles per kilogram.

30-27
00:39:39.633 --> 00:39:48.684
To obtain the total adsorbed amount, multiply that uptake by zero
point zero one zero kilogram, not by ten.

30-28
00:39:48.864 --> 00:39:54.368
The result is about zero point zero zero seven five six two mole
adsorbed.

30-29
00:39:54.548 --> 00:39:59.668
The gas contains about zero point zero zero two four three eight mole.

30-30
00:39:59.848 --> 00:40:04.712
Together these recover the total zero point zero one zero mole.

30-31
00:40:04.892 --> 00:40:10.265
Record the selected model, pressure, uptake, both amounts, and
residual.

30-32
00:40:10.445 --> 00:40:17.293
Before changing the model, save the calculated Langmuir case with its
interpretation and label.

30-33
00:40:17.473 --> 00:40:20.289
Then select Henry in the model menu.

30-34
00:40:20.469 --> 00:40:26.400
Enter a Henry coefficient of zero point two mole per kilogram per
kilopascal.

30-35
00:40:26.580 --> 00:40:35.241
This is the product of the Langmuir capacity and affinity, so the two
isotherms have the same limiting slope near zero pressure.

30-36
00:40:35.421 --> 00:40:41.453
Keep ten grams of adsorbent, the same volume, and the same total
inventory.

30-37
00:40:41.633 --> 00:40:44.193
Press Compare and solve vessel again.

30-38
00:40:44.373 --> 00:40:50.965
Matching the low pressure slope does not make the two models identical
over the full pressure range.

30-39
00:40:51.145 --> 00:40:58.292
The Henry calculation gives about four point one six five kilopascals
for case eleven B.

30-40
00:40:58.472 --> 00:41:09.117
It predicts about zero point zero zero eight three three zero mole
adsorbed and zero point zero zero one six seven zero mole in the gas.

30-41
00:41:09.297 --> 00:41:16.397
Its material balance closes, but that does not establish that the
approximation is physically adequate.

30-42
00:41:16.577 --> 00:41:24.998
At this pressure, the Langmuir product of affinity and pressure is
about zero point four two, which is not very small.

30-43
00:41:25.178 --> 00:41:33.840
The linear model stores more material on the adsorbent at a given
pressure, so it reaches the fixed inventory at a lower pressure.

30-44
00:41:34.020 --> 00:41:36.281
Save this second case separately.

30-45
00:41:36.461 --> 00:41:45.293
For case eleven C, return the selected model to Langmuir and increase
the dry adsorbent mass from ten to twenty grams.

30-46
00:41:45.473 --> 00:41:52.769
Keep the capacity, affinity, temperature, gas volume, and total
adsorbate inventory unchanged.

30-47
00:41:52.949 --> 00:42:03.616
Compare this case with the original Langmuir case, eleven A, so that
adsorbent mass is the only physical variable being changed in that
comparison.

30-48
00:42:03.796 --> 00:42:07.998
Notice that editing a field invalidates the old result.

30-49
00:42:08.178 --> 00:42:12.018
Recalculate before recording or exporting anything.

30-50
00:42:12.198 --> 00:42:20.348
We are adding adsorbent surface capacity to a vessel with the same
amount of adsorbate, not adding more adsorbate.

30-51
00:42:20.528 --> 00:42:28.037
With twenty grams of adsorbent, the Langmuir pressure falls to about
two point eight four five kilopascals.

30-52
00:42:28.217 --> 00:42:34.873
The total adsorbed amount increases to about zero point zero zero
eight eight five nine mole.

30-53
00:42:35.053 --> 00:42:42.626
Yet the uptake per kilogram decreases to about zero point four four
three zero mole per kilogram.

30-54
00:42:42.806 --> 00:42:45.174
These changes are consistent.

30-55
00:42:45.354 --> 00:42:51.452
There is more adsorbent, but the lower gas pressure leaves each
kilogram less heavily loaded.

30-56
00:42:51.632 --> 00:42:58.352
Multiply uptake by zero point zero two zero kilogram to recover the
adsorbed amount.

30-57
00:42:58.532 --> 00:43:06.916
Add the gas amount, about zero point zero zero one one four one mole,
and check the same total inventory.

30-58
00:43:07.096 --> 00:43:16.632
Save the third case, then widen the maximum chart pressure to one
hundred kilopascals and calculate again to inspect the model shapes.

30-59
00:43:16.812 --> 00:43:21.270
The Henry line continues upward without a finite capacity.

30-60
00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:27.210
The Langmuir curve bends toward the specified capacity of two moles
per kilogram.

30-61
00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:34.238
This helps explain the worksheet comparison at forty kilopascals,
where the two predictions differ strongly.

30-62
00:43:34.418 --> 00:43:39.432
A wider plotting range does not supply experimental validation.

30-63
00:43:39.612 --> 00:43:45.585
The Freundlich and BET curves use their own synthetic parameters and
domains.

30-64
00:43:45.765 --> 00:43:53.484
They are visible for context, but fitting them and interpreting
isosteric heat are optional extensions today.

30-65
00:43:53.664 --> 00:44:03.349
The Study workspace now contains three named model studies: Langmuir
with ten grams, Henry with ten grams, and Langmuir with twenty grams.

30-66
00:44:03.529 --> 00:44:10.270
For each one, use Your interpretation to record the balance and the
reason for the pressure change.

30-67
00:44:10.450 --> 00:44:17.640
Export model study downloads a calculated study and retains it in this
temporary comparison.

30-68
00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:23.324
Do not use Export data study from the separate fitting section for
this activity.

30-69
00:44:23.504 --> 00:44:34.917
The saved cases keep the original zero to ten kilopascal calculation
window even though we subsequently widened the current chart to
inspect the isotherm shapes.

30-70
00:44:35.097 --> 00:44:40.025
Select Download comparison to save these three model studies together.

30-71
00:44:40.205 --> 00:44:42.829
Read the recorded fields carefully.

30-72
00:44:43.009 --> 00:44:53.718
In this lab the equilibrium pressure is recorded in kilopascals, the
uptake in moles per kilogram, and the gas and adsorbed amounts in
moles.

30-73
00:44:53.898 --> 00:44:59.530
The vessel input mass is stored in kilograms and the volume in cubic
meters.

30-74
00:44:59.710 --> 00:45:05.812
This differs from some display units and from the pressure convention
in the gas solid export.

30-75
00:45:05.992 --> 00:45:12.093
Do not compare raw field values across different labs without checking
their definitions.

30-76
00:45:12.273 --> 00:45:15.388
A blank field is not automatically zero.

30-77
00:45:15.568 --> 00:45:22.288
To check that the study is reusable, clear the temporary comparison
only after saving the file.

30-78
00:45:22.468 --> 00:45:30.190
Under Load studies or a comparison JSON, choose the downloaded
adsorption comparison file.

30-79
00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:35.938
The file is read by the browser and the saved cases become available
to restore.

30-80
00:45:36.118 --> 00:45:39.873
Keep the actual file on your computer with the worksheet.

30-81
00:45:40.053 --> 00:45:45.024
An in memory comparison alone may disappear when you leave the page.

30-82
00:45:45.204 --> 00:45:56.842
Saving a screenshot is useful for discussion, but it cannot replace
the inputs, units, model selection, and interpretation needed to
reproduce the calculation.

30-83
00:45:57.022 --> 00:46:02.643
After import, select Restore beside case eleven A, Langmuir ten grams.

30-84
00:46:02.823 --> 00:46:11.685
Verify that the selected model, capacity, affinity, temperature,
inventory, and adsorbent mass have returned correctly.

30-85
00:46:11.865 --> 00:46:17.049
The plot range should also return to the saved zero to ten
kilopascals.

30-86
00:46:17.229 --> 00:46:21.367
Restoring inputs is not the final calculation step.

30-87
00:46:21.547 --> 00:46:25.643
Press Compare and solve vessel to calculate the current state.

30-88
00:46:25.823 --> 00:46:34.869
If you edit any parameter after restoration, calculate again before
treating the displayed result as evidence for that new case.

30-89
00:46:35.049 --> 00:46:46.846
The restored and recalculated Langmuir case again gives approximately
six point zero eight zero kilopascals and the same gas and adsorbed
amounts as before.

30-90
00:46:47.026 --> 00:46:53.106
This completes the full workflow from choosing a model to saving and
reopening the result.

30-91
00:46:53.286 --> 00:47:00.027
In worksheet section G, reproduce the three cases yourself and verify
each balance by hand.

30-92
00:47:00.207 --> 00:47:07.845
Explain why more adsorbent can increase the total adsorbed amount
while decreasing uptake per kilogram.

30-93
00:47:08.025 --> 00:47:12.611
State the absolute uptake basis and the ideal gas assumption.

30-94
00:47:12.791 --> 00:47:17.975
The next activity stop gives you time to create and check your own
evidence.

31-01
00:47:20.505 --> 00:47:25.092
A useful comparison needs more than a screenshot of a curve.

31-02
00:47:25.272 --> 00:47:31.949
Record the selected model, units and temperature because they define
the meaning of each parameter.

31-03
00:47:32.129 --> 00:47:37.271
Then record pressure and uptake separately from the gas and adsorbed
amounts.

31-04
00:47:37.451 --> 00:47:42.443
The uptake is measured per kilogram, while the vessel balance uses
moles.

31-05
00:47:42.623 --> 00:47:45.076
Check the mass conversion explicitly.

31-06
00:47:45.256 --> 00:47:54.024
The sum of gas moles and adsorbed moles should reproduce the chosen
inventory, within rounding and numerical precision.

31-07
00:47:54.204 --> 00:48:01.031
Keep a saved study and verify that reopening and recalculating it
reproduces the result.

31-08
00:48:01.211 --> 00:48:07.355
Remember that two models can share a low-pressure slope without
agreeing over the full range.

31-09
00:48:07.535 --> 00:48:19.285
Also, a correct numerical inventory balance verifies the calculation,
but it does not prove that the chosen isotherm accurately represents a
real material.

32-01
00:48:21.815 --> 00:48:28.535
Pause for about twenty-four minutes and complete G one through G four
on worksheet page five.

32-02
00:48:28.715 --> 00:48:33.238
Open Lab eleven and follow Lab guide pages three and four.

32-03
00:48:33.418 --> 00:48:39.370
Run three cases with the same temperature, accessible volume and total
inventory.

32-04
00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:43.945
Case eleven A uses Langmuir with ten grams of adsorbent.

32-05
00:48:44.125 --> 00:48:48.882
Case eleven B uses the matched Henry model with the same mass.

32-06
00:48:49.062 --> 00:48:53.521
Case eleven C returns to Langmuir and uses twenty grams.

32-07
00:48:53.701 --> 00:49:01.146
Record pressure, uptake, gas moles and adsorbed moles, then check each
balance with your calculator.

32-08
00:49:01.326 --> 00:49:05.486
Compare the findings with your predictions and inspect the graph.

32-09
00:49:05.666 --> 00:49:11.021
Save your studies, reopen one, verify its controls and calculate
again.

32-10
00:49:11.201 --> 00:49:18.859
Explain the difference between uptake per kilogram and total adsorbed
amount when interpreting the mass change.

33-01
00:49:21.389 --> 00:49:27.448
The Langmuir case with ten grams gives about six point zero eight zero
kilopascals.

33-02
00:49:27.628 --> 00:49:35.564
With the same inventory and mass, the matched Henry model gives about
four point one six five kilopascals.

33-03
00:49:35.744 --> 00:49:43.573
Henry predicts more uptake at a common positive pressure, so it
reaches this fixed inventory at a lower pressure.

33-04
00:49:43.753 --> 00:49:52.415
Returning to Langmuir and increasing mass to twenty grams lowers
pressure to about two point eight four five kilopascals.

33-05
00:49:52.595 --> 00:49:59.503
Uptake per kilogram decreases, but total adsorbed moles increase
because there is more adsorbent.

33-06
00:49:59.683 --> 00:50:03.139
Check the gas and adsorbed amounts in each row.

33-07
00:50:03.319 --> 00:50:06.369
Their sum is zero point zero one mole.

33-08
00:50:06.549 --> 00:50:16.135
The Henry result is useful for model sensitivity, but its finite b-P
value requires caution about the low-pressure approximation.

34-01
00:50:18.665 --> 00:50:25.104
The Lab includes additional models and analysis options, but they need
their own assumptions.

34-02
00:50:25.284 --> 00:50:32.196
Freundlich is an empirical expression over a stated pressure range and
has no saturation plateau.

34-03
00:50:32.376 --> 00:50:39.693
BET uses relative pressure and requires a justified fitting range and
reference pressure.

34-04
00:50:39.873 --> 00:50:46.871
Isosteric heat requires comparing pressures at the same absolute
loading across temperatures.

34-05
00:50:47.051 --> 00:50:51.787
Comparing arbitrary points on separate isotherms is not enough.

34-06
00:50:51.967 --> 00:50:58.751
Experimental fitting also needs measured data, uncertainty, units and
a clear objective.

34-07
00:50:58.931 --> 00:51:05.885
Today's examples use synthetic parameters to examine equilibrium and
inventory reasoning.

34-08
00:51:06.065 --> 00:51:11.484
They do not establish an adsorption mechanism or validate
extrapolation.

34-09
00:51:11.664 --> 00:51:22.672
You can explore the extensions later, but no new fit, BET calculation
or isosteric-heat calculation is required to complete this lesson.

34-10
00:51:22.852 --> 00:51:26.564
Choose one relevant limitation for your reflection.

35-01
00:51:29.094 --> 00:51:33.617
The final calculation transfers the method to new parameters.

35-02
00:51:33.797 --> 00:51:39.962
Keep the worked solutions closed and read H one through H four on
worksheet page six.

35-03
00:51:40.142 --> 00:51:48.803
For the gas-solid case, use three hundred and ten kelvin, two liters
and a sublimation pressure of five kilopascals.

35-04
00:51:48.983 --> 00:51:56.621
Compare volatile inventories of zero point zero zero two and zero
point zero zero eight moles.

35-05
00:51:56.801 --> 00:52:01.515
Add zero point zero zero one mole of inert gas to each.

35-06
00:52:01.695 --> 00:52:12.746
For adsorption, use a different synthetic material with saturation
uptake three moles per kilogram and b equal to zero point zero five
per kilopascal.

35-07
00:52:12.926 --> 00:52:22.526
At twenty kilopascals and three hundred and ten kelvin, use two liters
of accessible gas volume and five grams of adsorbent.

35-08
00:52:22.706 --> 00:52:26.269
Calculate the inventory required by that state.

36-01
00:52:28.799 --> 00:52:33.257
Pause for about twenty minutes and complete H one through H four.

36-02
00:52:33.437 --> 00:52:40.345
Use the equations from the earlier slides, but make your own
calculation before opening the answers.

36-03
00:52:40.525 --> 00:52:45.240
For gas-solid equilibrium, determine the gas capacity first.

36-04
00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:52.567
Then find gas and solid amounts, volatile partial pressure and total
pressure with the inert included.

36-05
00:52:52.747 --> 00:52:55.563
Check the species inventory in each case.

36-06
00:52:55.743 --> 00:53:00.180
For adsorption, evaluate the isotherm at the specified pressure.

36-07
00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:07.805
Convert five grams to kilograms, then calculate gas moles, adsorbed
moles and total inventory.

36-08
00:53:07.985 --> 00:53:13.959
Predict what happens to pressure if adsorbent mass doubles while that
inventory stays fixed.

36-09
00:53:14.139 --> 00:53:16.976
An exact new pressure is not required.

36-10
00:53:17.156 --> 00:53:25.071
Finally, state one shared assumption and one difference between bulk
gas-solid equilibrium and surface adsorption.

37-01
00:53:27.601 --> 00:53:34.193
The gas capacity is approximately zero point zero zero three eight
eight zero moles.

37-02
00:53:34.373 --> 00:53:39.023
The smaller volatile inventory therefore completely vaporizes.

37-03
00:53:39.203 --> 00:53:51.150
The larger inventory leaves about zero point zero zero four one two
zero moles of solid and maintains a volatile partial pressure of five
kilopascals.

37-04
00:53:51.330 --> 00:53:56.407
The inert contribution is about one point two eight nine kilopascals.

37-05
00:53:56.587 --> 00:53:59.382
Add it to obtain the total pressures shown.

37-06
00:53:59.562 --> 00:54:05.941
For adsorption, uptake at twenty kilopascals is one point five moles
per kilogram.

37-07
00:54:06.121 --> 00:54:15.536
Gas moles are about zero point zero one five five one nine, and
adsorbed moles are zero point zero zero seven five.

37-08
00:54:15.716 --> 00:54:20.473
Their sum is about zero point zero two three zero one nine moles.

37-09
00:54:20.653 --> 00:54:24.963
Doubling adsorbent mass at that fixed inventory lowers pressure.

38-01
00:54:27.493 --> 00:54:32.613
Use these four questions whenever you approach a new equilibrium
problem.

38-02
00:54:32.793 --> 00:54:35.704
First, identify the physical process.

38-03
00:54:35.884 --> 00:54:42.135
Bulk phase transfer, surface uptake and chemical reaction require
different descriptions.

38-04
00:54:42.315 --> 00:54:44.357
Second, state the constraints.

38-05
00:54:44.537 --> 00:54:51.065
Temperature and pressure are not interchangeable with temperature and
accessible volume.

38-06
00:54:51.245 --> 00:55:01.018
Third, identify the model and its conventions, including standard
states, gas ideality, uptake basis, units and pressure range.

38-07
00:55:01.198 --> 00:55:08.643
Finally, use material balances to determine amounts and check whether
the proposed phases are actually present.

38-08
00:55:08.823 --> 00:55:17.250
A solubility composition, a sublimation pressure or an isotherm
loading supplies only part of the description.

38-09
00:55:17.430 --> 00:55:20.182
None replaces an inventory balance.

38-10
00:55:20.362 --> 00:55:29.429
In your reflection, report a numerical result with its units, one
supporting check and the assumptions needed for the interpretation.

38-11
00:55:29.609 --> 00:55:33.790
That combination makes the result useful and reproducible.

39-01
00:55:36.320 --> 00:55:45.045
